{
  "$schema": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nobrega8/mbmap-library/main/schema/mbmap-2.schema.json",
  "mbmap": 2,
  "vendor": "Trinity",
  "model": "GOBLIN-PLUS",
  "models": [
    "GOBLIN-PLUS"
  ],
  "vendor_website": "trinityenergy.co.in",
  "description": "Multifunction power analyser with RS485 Modbus RTU: live values, power factor, powers, frequency, temperature, import/export/net energies and demands",
  "revision": 1,
  "source": "Trinity Energy Systems, GOBLIN-PLUS Operational Manual, Appendix (Modbus address map)",
  "sources": [
    {
      "title": "GOBLIN-PLUS Operational Manual",
      "note": "section 'Modbus RTU on RS485 Port' and the Appendix address map; 'Energy parameter reading' and 'Defining multiplication factor'"
    }
  ],
  "notes": [
    "Trinity GOBLIN-PLUS. The live value, energy and demand block of the Appendix (200 to 445); the minimum/maximum, event and sag/swell logs from 446 are not in this map. The register map differs from the older Goblin (see trinity/goblin) from address 226 on.",
    "The manual gives the addresses in decimal but no example frame. This map sends address 200 of the table as 199 (address_base 1), as the Trinity maps of this library do; if a device answers one register off, use address_base 0.",
    "Function 03 reads the registers; at most 254 bytes per request. 9600, 19200 or 38400 baud, 8N1. Names are those of the 3P4W column; registers marked as not used in 3P3W or 1P2W read zero there.",
    "Values are integers multiplied by the factor in the table (the scale here): x100 for voltages, currents, powers, frequency and temperature, x1000 for power factor.",
    "Energies are split in two registers: energy = KxH1 x 10,000,000 + KxH2 / 100, and the net energies are multiplied by the sign register (+1 or -1). The worked example in the manual uses addresses two lower than the table (265 for KWH1 import); this map follows the table.",
    "Not tested on a device."
  ],
  "byte_order": "big",
  "word_order": "big",
  "address_base": 1,
  "x_status": "official",
